  <h1>How to learn Korean: a simple plan for beginners</h1>
  <p class="lede">You can learn Korean from zero with a simple plan: learn the Hangul alphabet first, then build core words and sentences, and speak a little every day. Korean looks hard from the outside, but its writing system is one of the most logical in the world — most learners can read Hangul within a week or two.</p>

  <div class="ad-slot" role="complementary" aria-label="Advertisement"><span>Advertisement</span></div>

  <h2>Step 1 — Learn Hangul, the Korean alphabet</h2>
  <p>Hangul has 24 basic letters: 14 consonants and 10 vowels. Unlike Chinese characters, each letter stands for a sound, and letters join into syllable blocks — for example, 한 (han) is three letters in one block. Spend your first week only on Hangul: write each letter, say its sound, and practise reading simple words like 안녕하세요 (annyeonghaseyo, hello).</p>
  <p>Do not rely on romanisation (writing Korean sounds with English letters). It feels easier at first, but it hides the real sounds and slows you down later. Every Korean learning resource uses Hangul, so learning it early unlocks everything else.</p>
  <h2>Step 2 — Learn your first 100 core words</h2>
  <p>Start with words you will actually use: greetings, numbers, food, family, time and everyday verbs. Learn them in sentences, not alone — for example, instead of just memorising 밥 (bap, rice), learn 밥을 먹어요 (babeul meogeoyo, I eat rice). Words in sentences stick far better than words on a list.</p>
  <p>Add 5–10 new words a day and review the old ones. A small daily habit beats a big weekend cram session, because your brain needs sleep to move new words into long-term memory.</p>
  <h2>Step 3 — Learn the basic sentence pattern</h2>
  <p>Korean sentences usually follow subject–object–verb order, the opposite of English. So “I eat rice” becomes 저는 밥을 먹어요 (jeoneun babeul meogeoyo) — literally “I rice eat”. Once this clicks, making sentences becomes much easier.</p>
  <p>Learn the two most useful particles first: 은/는 (eun/neun) marks the topic of the sentence, and 을/를 (eul/reul) marks the object. You do not need to master every particle before you speak — learn them one at a time, as you meet them.</p>
  <h2>Step 4 — Listen and speak every day</h2>
  <p>Listen to Korean daily, even passively: K-dramas, K-pop, podcasts or YouTube. At first you will only catch a word here and there, and that is normal. Your ear is learning the rhythm and sounds of the language.</p>
  <p>Speak from week one, even if it is just reading sentences aloud or repeating lines from a show. Speaking with a tutor is the fastest way to fix pronunciation early, especially the sounds that do not exist in English, like the difference between ㄱ, ㅋ and ㄲ.</p>
  <h2>Step 5 — Read, write and keep going</h2>
  <p>When you can read Hangul, start reading simple things: children’s books, song lyrics, signs, or short social media posts. Write a few sentences a day — a diary in Korean, even three lines, forces you to use the grammar you have learned.</p>
  <p>Progress in Korean is not a straight line. There will be weeks when it feels slow. The learners who reach a good level are not the cleverest ones — they are the ones who kept a small daily habit for months.</p>
  <h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Skipping Hangul and depending on romanisation — it caps your progress early.</li>
    <li>Trying to memorise hundreds of words before speaking a single sentence.</li>
    <li>Learning only formal, textbook Korean and never practising casual speech.</li>
    <li>Ignoring pronunciation — Korean has sounds that look similar in English letters but are different in Korean.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Practical tips</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Learn Hangul in your first week — it is the single best investment you can make.</li>
    <li>Study 20–30 minutes a day, every day, rather than 3 hours once a week.</li>
    <li>Learn verbs in their polite -요 form first, since that is what you will use most.</li>
    <li>Watch one K-drama episode with Korean subtitles and write down five new words.</li>
  </ul>

