  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>

  <details><summary>How long does it take to learn Korean?</summary><p>It depends on your goal and how much you study. With 30 minutes a day, most learners can hold a simple conversation after 3–6 months, and reach a comfortable everyday level in 1–2 years. Korean is often rated as a harder language for English speakers, mainly because of the grammar and vocabulary, but Hangul itself is quick to learn.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Is Korean hard to learn?</summary><p>Some parts are hard — the grammar order is different, and there are sounds and speech levels that do not exist in English. Other parts are easy: Hangul is logical, verbs do not change for person (I, you, he all use the same form), and there is no grammatical gender. The difficulty is real but very manageable with a steady plan.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Do I need to learn Hangul, or can I use romanisation?</summary><p>Learn Hangul. Romanisation is inconsistent — the same Korean sound is written different ways in different books — and it hides sounds that matter. Most learners can read Hangul slowly within one to two weeks of short daily practice.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Can I learn Korean by myself?</summary><p>Yes, you can get a long way alone with apps, books and videos. But speaking needs a partner. A tutor helps you fix pronunciation, practise real conversations and stay accountable — that is the part self-study struggles with.</p></details>
